From 7064ef4ac7dd9fb5eb87487acd6378576b5f2d5c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dj-oyu <68707227+dj-oyu@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 22:42:50 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] fix(quickjs): resolving-functions double-free on allocation
 failure

js_create_resolving_functions() built the resolve/reject pair for a
promise capability and, when the SECOND function's allocation failed,
freed resolving_funcs[0] (already built by the first iteration) without
clearing the slot. Every caller that frees resolving_funcs[0]/[1]
unconditionally on its own failure path -- js_async_function_call is the
one that bites here -- then double-frees it: ASan heap-use-after-free.

Stage A2 (8511f6b) found and worked around this locally in
flat_async_call, on the theory that upstream's js_async_function_call has
the same bug but is unreachable behind the C-stack-depth test. That
theory is wrong for this fork: js_vm_flat_callable() only fast-paths a
DIRECT bytecode call to an async function, so any call that reaches one
through JS_Call from native glue (Function.prototype.apply/call/bind,
Reflect.apply, a Proxy trap, a builtin's callback) still runs through
js_async_function_call regardless of CONFIG_POCKET_VM_FLATCALLS -- reachable
at recursion depth 1 on the shipped default build, confirmed with
`f.apply(null, [])` and --fail-alloc.

Fix at the root instead: js_create_resolving_functions's fail: label now
sets resolving_funcs[0] = JS_UNDEFINED after freeing it, so every caller's
contract ("failure leaves live values or JS_UNDEFINED, never something
already freed") actually holds. flat_async_call's local workaround is
removed as redundant. Also closes a second, previously unnoticed instance
of the same bug in module evaluation (JSModuleDef.resolving_funcs, freed
unconditionally by the module finalizer).

Regression: tools/vmtest/corpus/oom_resolving_functions.js, blessed on
asan-recur (upstream's call shape) and confirmed byte-identical on every
other build variant including the default flat-calls one.

Gates: build.sh all/all-alloca/all-recur/all-flat (8 variants) + run.sh
each -- 47/47 corpus; run.sh --force-yield -- 3 pass/44 fail (L2c-expected,
unchanged shape); test262.py --variant asan -j8 -- 7501 pass/194 fail/0
regressions; stack_probe.sh 2000 o2 SELFTEST=1 -- 0.000 NOT_PROPORTIONAL;
budget_probe.sh o2-flat and o2-recur -- checks=11 failed=0 each; vmalloc
verify_all.sh -- G6 all traces verify clean, all faults caught.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XW6rzPVz92arombQ6VTgZH
---
 components/quickjs-ng/quickjs-ng/quickjs.c    | 42 ++++++++++++-----
 .../vmtest/corpus/oom_resolving_functions.js  | 46 +++++++++++++++++++
 .../expected/oom_resolving_functions.txt      |  3 ++
 3 files changed, 79 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 tools/vmtest/corpus/oom_resolving_functions.js
 create mode 100644 tools/vmtest/expected/oom_resolving_functions.txt

diff --git a/components/quickjs-ng/quickjs-ng/quickjs.c b/components/quickjs-ng/quickjs-ng/quickjs.c
index 6d48ae4..b14bf7a 100644
--- a/components/quickjs-ng/quickjs-ng/quickjs.c
+++ b/components/quickjs-ng/quickjs-ng/quickjs.c
@@ -21421,17 +21421,15 @@ flat_async_call: {
             s->resolving_funcs[1] = JS_UNDEFINED;
             promise = JS_NewPromiseCapability(ctx, s->resolving_funcs);
             if (JS_IsException(promise)) {
-                // Not upstream's fail: verbatim. js_create_resolving_functions
-                // frees resolving_funcs[0] when the SECOND function's
-                // allocation fails but leaves the dead value in the slot,
-                // and js_async_function_free0 would free it again (ASan:
-                // heap-use-after-free, seen on the host profile, where D38
-                // makes heap exhaustion the normal end of a deep async
-                // recursion). Upstream's js_async_function_call has the same
-                // two lines and the same latent bug; it is unreachable there
-                // because the C-stack test fires long before the heap.
-                s->resolving_funcs[0] = JS_UNDEFINED;
-                s->resolving_funcs[1] = JS_UNDEFINED;
+                // Upstream's fail: verbatim (matches js_async_function_call
+                // below). js_create_resolving_functions now guarantees
+                // s->resolving_funcs[0]/[1] are never left holding a value
+                // it has already freed -- see its fail: label -- so
+                // js_async_function_free's unconditional free of both slots
+                // is safe here even when the second resolving function's
+                // allocation is what failed. D38 makes this reachable in
+                // practice: a synchronous deep async recursion ends in heap
+                // exhaustion here, not in a C-stack test.
                 js_async_function_free(rt, s);
                 goto exception;
             }
@@ -57026,9 +57024,29 @@ static int js_create_resolving_functions(JSContext *ctx,
         if (!s) {
             JS_FreeValue(ctx, obj);
 fail:
-
+            // Contract with every caller (JS_NewPromiseCapability and its
+            // users, js_promise_resolve_thenable_job, the promise
+            // constructor): on failure resolving_funcs[0] and
+            // resolving_funcs[1] are always left either holding a live
+            // value or JS_UNDEFINED, never a value this function has
+            // already freed. resolving_funcs[i] itself is never written on
+            // this path (the assignment at the bottom of the loop runs only
+            // after a successful malloc), so it keeps whatever the caller
+            // put there before the call -- callers here pre-initialize both
+            // slots to JS_UNDEFINED. resolving_funcs[0] is the one slot this
+            // function itself can poison: when i==1 fails, index 0 already
+            // holds a real object from the first iteration, and freeing it
+            // here without clearing it left a dangling value in an output
+            // slot the caller believes is still valid. A caller that frees
+            // unconditionally on failure (upstream's js_async_function_call
+            // among them) then double-frees it -- ASan heap-use-after-free,
+            // reachable once a caller stops gating that free on "did this
+            // call fail" and starts doing it unconditionally, which is
+            // exactly what an unwind path through js_async_function_free
+            // does.
             if (i != 0) {
                 JS_FreeValue(ctx, resolving_funcs[0]);
+                resolving_funcs[0] = JS_UNDEFINED;
             }
             ret = -1;
             break;
diff --git a/tools/vmtest/corpus/oom_resolving_functions.js b/tools/vmtest/corpus/oom_resolving_functions.js
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4795841
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/vmtest/corpus/oom_resolving_functions.js
@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+// vmrun-flags: --fail-alloc 1353
+// Regression for the resolving-functions double-free (reports/upstream/
+// quickjs-ng-resolving-functions-double-free.md). js_create_resolving_
+// functions() used to leave a dangling value in resolving_funcs[0] when the
+// SECOND resolve function's allocation failed, and js_async_function_call's
+// fail: path frees both slots unconditionally -- so an allocation failure at
+// exactly the wrong point turned into an ASan heap-use-after-free instead of
+// a clean thrown error.
+//
+// f.apply() is deliberate, not incidental: js_vm_flat_callable() only
+// special-cases a DIRECT bytecode call (OP_call/OP_call_method) to an async
+// function. Every call that reaches JS_Call() from native glue --
+// Function.prototype.apply/call/bind, Reflect.apply, a Proxy trap, a
+// builtin's callback -- goes through js_async_function_call regardless of
+// CONFIG_POCKET_VM_FLATCALLS, so this bug is reachable in the SHIPPED
+// default build (flatcalls on) at recursion depth 1, not only through the
+// deep synchronous recursion that flat calls turn into heap exhaustion
+// (docs/vm-L2-design.md sec.12.2 D38, see budget_probe.sh's
+// deep_async_recursion). A plain `f()` here would go flat and miss the
+// js_async_function_call path entirely on the shipped config.
+//
+// --fail-alloc 1353 targets the js_malloc(sizeof(JSPromiseFunctionData))
+// call for the SECOND (reject) resolving function inside
+// js_create_resolving_functions, reached from JS_NewPromiseCapability inside
+// js_async_function_call. Like every --fail-alloc corpus case, this attempt
+// number is a snapshot of the current allocation sequence up to that call:
+// it depends on how many allocations context bootstrap performs before the
+// script runs, which shifts if bootstrap grows or shrinks. If this file
+// starts passing trivially (no exception, output changes) or with a
+// mismatched failure site, the number needs to be relocated by instrumenting
+// js_create_resolving_functions's attempt count before it -- do not just move
+// the number until something fails.
+//
+// Blessed on asan-recur AND checked to match under the default (flatcalls)
+// variant: the whole point of the fix is that the failure is caught the same
+// way, at the same JS-observable point, on both the path that keeps the C
+// recursion for a call and the path that flattens it (this call never
+// flattens either way, see above).
+async function f() {}
+try {
+  f.apply(null, []);
+  print("no-throw");
+} catch (e) {
+  print("caught", e instanceof Error, e.constructor.name);
+}
+print("done");
diff --git a/tools/vmtest/expected/oom_resolving_functions.txt b/tools/vmtest/expected/oom_resolving_functions.txt
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..74161f0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/vmtest/expected/oom_resolving_functions.txt
@@ -0,0 +1,3 @@
+caught true InternalError
+done
+exit=0
